# Guide: the first migration

Run this once before starting the app for the first time — it generates
the initial migration from the models in `src/{{ package_name }}/models.py`
and applies it, creating the schema:

```
uv run flask --app src/{{ package_name }}/main.py db migrate -m "describe your change"
uv run flask --app src/{{ package_name }}/main.py db upgrade
```

No `flask db init` step first — `migrations/` (`env.py`, `alembic.ini`,
`script.py.mako`, `versions/`) is already part of the generated project,
unlike most Flask-Migrate walkthroughs that start from an empty project.
Running `db init` against it would fail, and it isn't needed here — see
`references/gotchas.md`.

## What each command actually does

1. `db migrate -m "..."` diffs your models against the (currently
   empty) migration history and writes a new file under
   `migrations/versions/`. This step only writes a file — the database
   itself is untouched until the next command.
2. `db upgrade` applies that file (and any other unapplied revisions) to
   the real database configured by `SQLALCHEMY_DATABASE_URI` — this is
   the step that actually creates the tables.

Until both commands have run, the database has no tables and the app
will fail on the first query that touches one.

## Review before applying

Open the file `db migrate` just wrote under `migrations/versions/`
before running `db upgrade` — autogenerate is a diffing heuristic (see
the `alembic` skill), not a guarantee it captured exactly what you
intended. This matters even more on the very first migration, since it
typically creates every table in one file — a good moment to check
column types, nullability, and defaults all came out the way the models
say they should.

## Repeat both commands on every model change

Whenever you add, remove, or change a field on a model in
`src/{{ package_name }}/models.py`:

```
uv run flask --app src/{{ package_name }}/main.py db migrate -m "describe your change"
uv run flask --app src/{{ package_name }}/main.py db upgrade
```

Same two commands, same order, every time — there's no separate "first
migration" mechanism versus "later migration" mechanism, only the first
one happens to run against an empty history. If `db migrate` reports
"No changes in schema detected" and you know you changed a model, see
`references/gotchas.md` for the most likely cause.

## Rolling back

`uv run flask --app src/{{ package_name }}/main.py db downgrade` reverts
the single most recently applied revision. Run it again to step back
further. This runs the migration file's `downgrade()` function, so it's
only as safe as that function is — for a destructive change (e.g. a
dropped column), downgrading doesn't bring the data back.
